//! # Supervision
//!
//! A supervisor owns named children and applies a restart policy when a child
//! traps. `one_for_one` restarts only the failed child; `one_for_all` restarts
//! every child in the supervisor. The intensity window limits repeated
//! failures before the supervisor itself escalates.
//!
//! ```hew
//! actor Worker {
//!     var value: i64 = 0;
//!     receive fn add(n: i64) { value = value + n; }
//!     receive fn read() -> i64 { value }
//!     receive fn fail() { panic("worker failed"); }
//! }
//!
//! supervisor App {
//!     strategy: one_for_one;
//!     intensity: 3 within 60s;
//!     child worker: Worker;
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let app = spawn App;
//!     let worker = app.worker;
//!     worker.add(1);
//!     worker.fail();
//!     sleep(50ms);
//!     let replacement = app.worker;
//!     match await replacement.read() {
//!         .Ok(value) => println(value),
//!         .Err(_) => println("read failed"),
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! A child lookup after restart resolves the current child through the
//! supervisor (`app.worker`). A handle captured before a same-key restart is
//! stale and must not be reused. Restarted actors are reconstructed from their
//! declaration and initialization inputs; mutable runtime state is not
//! preserved unless it is stored elsewhere.
//!
//! `await_restart supervisor.child` is the blocking restart barrier for a
//! contextless caller such as `main`; it returns a fresh typed child handle.
//! Inside an actor, use the normal suspension-aware `await` forms.
